  <section number="11.7" title="Strategy for Testing Series">
    <figure>
      <credit>Copyright © Cengage Learning. All Rights Reserved</credit>
    </figure>
    <content>
      <paragraph>
        We now have several ways of testing a series for convergence or divergence; the problem is to decide which test to use on which series. In this respect, testing series is similar to integrating functions. Again, there are no hard and fast rules about which test to apply to a given series, but you may find the following advice of some use.
      </paragraph>
      <paragraph>
        It is not wise to apply a list of the tests in a specific order until one finally works. That would be a waste of time and effort. Instead, as with integration, the main strategy is to classify the series according to its <em>form</em>.
      </paragraph>
      <list ordered="true">
        <item>
          <paragraph>
            <strong>Test for Divergence</strong> If you can see that <math display="inline">$$\lim_{n\rightarrow\infty}a_{n}$$</math> may be different from <math display="inline">$$0$$</math> , then apply the Test for Divergence.
          </paragraph>
        </item>
        <item>
          <paragraph>
            <math display="inline">$$\mathbf{p}$$</math> If the series is of the form <math display="inline">$$\sum 1 \slash n^{p}$$</math> , then it is a <math display="inline">$$p$$</math>-series, which we know to be convergent if <math display="inline">$$p &gt; 1$$</math> and divergent if <math display="inline">$$p \leqslant 1$$</math> .
          </paragraph>
        </item>
        <item>
          <paragraph>
            <strong>Geometric Series</strong> If the series has the form <math display="inline">$$\sum ar^{n - 1}$$</math> or <math display="inline">$$\sum ar^{n}$$</math> , then it is a geometric series, which converges if <math display="inline">$$|r| &lt; 1$$</math> and diverges if <math display="inline">$$|r| \geqslant 1$$</math> . Some preliminary algebraic manipulation may be required to bring the series into this form.
          </paragraph>
        </item>
        <item>
          <paragraph>
            <strong>Comparison Tests</strong> If the series has a form that is similar to a <math display="inline">$$p$$</math>-series or a geometric series, then one of the comparison tests should be considered. In particular, if <math display="inline">$$a_{n}$$</math> is a rational function or an algebraic function of <math display="inline">$$n$$</math> (involving roots of polynomials), then the series should be compared with a <math display="inline">$$p$$</math>-series. Notice that most of the series in Exercises 11.4 have this form. (The value of <math display="inline">$$p$$</math> should be chosen as in Section 11.4 by keeping only the highest powers of <math display="inline">$$n$$</math> in the numerator and denominator.) The comparison tests apply only to series with positive terms, but if <math display="inline">$$\sum a_{n}$$</math> has some negative terms, then we can apply a comparison test to <math display="inline">$$\sum\left| a_{n} \right|$$</math> and test for absolute convergence.
          </paragraph>
        </item>
        <item>
          <paragraph>
            <strong>Alternating Series Test</strong> If the series is of the form <math display="inline">$$\sum\left( {- 1} \right)^{n - 1}b_{n}$$</math> or <math display="inline">$$\sum\left( {- 1} \right)^{n}b_{n}$$</math> , then the Alternating Series Test is an obvious possibility. Note that if <math display="inline">$$\sum b_{n}$$</math> converges, then the given series is absolutely convergent and therefore convergent.
          </paragraph>
        </item>
        <item>
          <paragraph>
            <strong>Ratio Test</strong> Series that involve factorials or other products (including a constant raised to the <math display="inline">$$n$$</math> th power) are often conveniently tested using the Ratio Test. Bear in mind that <math display="inline">$$\left. \left| {a_{n + 1} \slash a_{n}} \right|\rightarrow 1 \right.$$</math> as <math display="inline">$$\left. n\rightarrow\infty \right.$$</math> for all <math display="inline">$$p$$</math>-series and therefore all rational or algebraic functions of <math display="inline">$$n$$</math> . Thus the Ratio Test should not be used for such series.
          </paragraph>
        </item>
        <item>
          <paragraph>
            <strong>Root Test</strong> If <math display="inline">$$a_{n}$$</math> is of the form <math display="inline">$$\left( b_{n} \right)^{n}$$</math> , then the Root Test may be useful.
          </paragraph>
        </item>
        <item>
          <paragraph>
            <strong>Integral Test</strong> If <math display="inline">$$a_{n} = f(n)$$</math> , where <math display="inline">$${\int_{1}^{\infty}f}(x)dx$$</math> is easily evaluated, then the Integral Test is effective (assuming the hypotheses of this test are satisfied).
          </paragraph>
        </item>
      </list>
      <paragraph>
        In the following examples we don’t work out all the details but simply indicate which tests should be used.
      </paragraph>
    </content>
    <example number="1">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>
          <math display="inline">$$\sum\limits_{n = 1}^{\infty}\frac{n - 1}{2n + 1}$$</math>
        </paragraph>
        <paragraph>
          Since <math display="inline">$$\left. a_{n}\rightarrow\frac{1}{2} \neq 0 \right.$$</math> as <math display="inline">$$\left. n\rightarrow\infty \right.$$</math> , we should use the Test for Divergence.
        </paragraph>
      </problem>
    </example>
    <example number="2">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>
          <math display="inline">$$\sum\limits_{n = 1}^{\infty}\frac{\sqrt{n^{3} + 1}}{3n^{3} + 4n^{2} + 2}$$</math>
        </paragraph>
        <paragraph>
          Since <math display="inline">$$a_{n}$$</math> is an algebraic function of <math display="inline">$$n$$</math> , we compare the given series with a <math display="inline">$$p$$</math>-series. The comparison series for the Limit Comparison Test is <math display="inline">$$\sum b_{n}$$</math> , where
        </paragraph>
        <paragraph>
          <math display="block">$$b_{n} = \frac{\sqrt{n^{3}}}{3n^{3}} = \frac{n^{3 \slash 2}}{3n^{3}} = \frac{1}{3n^{3 \slash 2}}$$</math>
        </paragraph>
      </problem>
    </example>
    <example number="3">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>
          <math display="inline">$$\sum\limits_{n = 1}^{\infty}ne^{- n^{2}}$$</math>
        </paragraph>
        <paragraph>
          Since the integral <math display="inline">$${\int_{1}^{\infty}{xe^{- x^{2}}}}dx$$</math> is easily evaluated, we use the Integral Test. The Ratio Test also works.
        </paragraph>
      </problem>
    </example>
    <example number="4">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>
          <math display="inline">$$\sum\limits_{n = 1}^{\infty}\left( {- 1} \right)^{n}\frac{n^{2}}{n^{4} + 1}$$</math>
        </paragraph>
        <paragraph>
          Since the series is alternating, we use the Alternating Series Test. We can also observe that <math display="inline">$$\left. \Sigma \middle| a_{n} \right|$$</math> converges (compare to <math display="inline">$$\Sigma 1/n^{2}$$</math> so the given series converges absolutely and hence converges.
        </paragraph>
      </problem>
    </example>
    <example number="5">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>
          <math display="inline">$$\sum\limits_{k = 1}^{\infty}\frac{2^{k}}{k!}$$</math>
        </paragraph>
        <paragraph>
          Since the series involves <math display="inline">$$k!$$</math> , we use the Ratio Test.
        </paragraph>
      </problem>
    </example>
    <example number="6">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>
          <math display="inline">$$\sum\limits_{n = 1}^{\infty}\frac{1}{2 + 3^{n}}$$</math>
        </paragraph>
        <paragraph>
          Since the series is closely related to the geometric series <math display="inline">$$\sum 1 \slash 3^{n}$$</math> , we use the Direct Comparison Test or the Limit Comparison Test.
        </paragraph>
      </problem>
    </example>
  </section>
